<Page title={"Methodology"} description={"Editorial method: sources, the distinction between original analysis and external publications, corrections."} lang="en">
	<h1 class="page-title">Methodology</h1>
	<p class="page-lede">How this site produces and distinguishes its content.</p>
	<div class="page-body">
<h2>What this site publishes</h2>
<p>This site is not a news outlet: nothing is published here first. It catalogues work that appeared elsewhere and gives it context. Two sets:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Publications</strong>: a portfolio of pieces published in other outlets. Each entry summarises the piece, names the outlet and the date, and links to the published version: the article is read where it was published.</li>
<li><strong>Research</strong>: academic work and expert papers, presented by status (published, accepted, forthcoming), never conflated.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Three statuses, never mixed</h2>
<p>Within publications, an <strong>op-ed</strong> is a piece written and signed by Romain Blachier. An <strong>interview</strong> is an exchange reported by a journalist. A <strong>quote</strong> is a third-party article in which he is cited. All three are labelled on every entry: a quote is never presented as a byline.</p>
<h2>What is not published here</h2>
<p>Articles under peer review are not listed while review is under way: in a double-blind process, announcing them publicly would compromise anonymity. Assignment deliverables handed to a client remain confidential; only their nature is described.</p>
<h2>Sources</h2>
<p>The work relies on primary sources (regulation, institutional reports, sector data, interviews) that are cited and verifiable.</p>
<h2>Corrections</h2>
<p>Any reported error is corrected transparently. See the Corrections page.</p>
	</div>
</Page>
